# The build directories are large, are rebuilt inside the image anyway, and one of them
# holds a mounted disc image. Keeping them out of the context is what makes the build quick.
[Bb]uild/
[Bb]uild-*/

# Game data belongs to whoever runs the image, never to the image itself. assets/ is the
# default OPENTS_ASSETS mount point for an OpenTS-Assets "build/web" tree; compose.yaml
# mounts it read only at run time and it must never enter the build context.
Run/
*.iso
/assets/

# The engine signing key reaches the build only through --mount=type=secret, never the
# context, so `COPY . .` can never fold it into a layer. The same goes for anything else
# holding a credential: tauri/.env carries signing and notarisation values, and a build
# that cannot see it cannot bake it into one.
.sigkey
*.sigkey
.env
*/.env

# Published installers. nginx serves these from a read-only mount at run time, the way it
# does the asset tree, so the image has no use for them either.
/downloads/

# The native shell, which this image neither builds nor serves. It is by far the largest
# thing in the tree, and every change under it would otherwise invalidate `COPY . .` and
# rebuild the whole engine.
tauri/

.git/
.github/
.vscode/
.cursor/
.claude/
manual/
docs/
